<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hasanlu Lovers</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 800 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hasanlu Lovers are two human skeletons found in 1972 at Hasanlu Tepe in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, lying together in an apparent embrace in a bin where they died when the citadel was destroyed around 800 BCE. They were excavated by the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> Museum's team under Robert Dyson. Later study suggests that both were probably male.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Hasanlu lies in the Qadar River valley on the southern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-urmia">Lake Urmia</a> and was occupied from the sixth millennium BCE until its destruction by fire. Excavation revealed burnt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mudbrick</a> walls, thick layers of ash and about 246 skeletons of people of many ages left where they were killed in the streets and buildings; some lay in groups with head wounds and severed limbs, which suggests mass executions. The attackers are unknown: most scholars name the Urartians, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> was also active in the region. The city had no writing, and its original name is unknown.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The pair lay in a plaster-lined brick bin, facing each other, the left skeleton reaching towards the other with its right hand, with nothing else but a stone slab under one head. Neither shows a fatal wound; the archaeologist Oscar White Muscarella thought a hole in the right skull was made by a workman's pickaxe. One view is that they crawled into the bin, probably covered then, to hide, and the probable cause of death is asphyxiation when burning debris sealed them in.</p><p>The right skeleton, SK 335, was a young adult of about 19 to 22, identified as male mainly from the pelvis. The left, SK 336, was about 30 to 35 and was first identified as female, but its cranium is male and its pelvis mixed, and the anthropologists Page Selinsky and Janet Monge, comparing ancient DNA with the skeletal evidence, concluded that both were male. Oxygen isotopes show that both were born and raised near Hasanlu. The pair were shown at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penn-museum">Penn Museum</a> from 1974 until the mid-1980s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The name Hasanlu Lovers comes from their intimate pose, and before the DNA tests they were taken for a man and a woman; Muscarella, a co-director of the excavations in the 1960s, said, &quot;I knew at first sight who was the female&quot;. Some researchers criticise the sensationalism around the find. Killgrove and Geller argue that modern culture primes viewers to expect a heterosexual couple, and Geller warns against projecting present ideas of sex and gender onto the past: the true relationship between the two remains unknown.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hasanlu Lovers” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Penn Museum excavations under Robert Dyson (1956 to 1977) found hundreds of bodies, including the pair known as the '<b>Hasanlu Lovers</b>'.</blockquote><a href="/state/hasanlu">Hasanlu</a></li></ul></section>
